<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gortyn Code</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 450 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Great Code of Gortyn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gortyn Code is the longest surviving ancient Greek law code, inscribed in the Doric dialect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> on a curved stone wall at Gortyn around 450 BCE. Its twelve columns, written in alternating directions, set out rules on marriage, divorce, inheritance, adoption, property and the status of slaves and serfs, giving a rare view of family law in a Greek city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gortyn-code.jpg" alt="Gortyn Code" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Federico_Halbherr_Gortyn.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The code, also called the Great Code, is known only from the inscription itself, cut on the circular wall of what may have been a bouleuterion or other public building in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agora">agora</a> of Gortyn. The building was 30 m in diameter; the twelve surviving columns of text are 10 m long and 1.5 m high and hold some 600 lines. It has been dated to the first half of the 5th century BCE.</p><p>It belongs to a tradition of Cretan law, the only substantial body of Greek law from antiquity found outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. Earlier laws, of about 600 to 525 BCE, were inscribed on the steps and walls of the temple of Apollo Pythios, and the Great Code is one of a series written on walls in or near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agora">agora</a> between about 525 and 400 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The code distinguishes the free, the serf, the slave and the foreigner. Anyone bringing a suit is forbidden to seize the accused before trial, on pain of a fine that varies with the status of the person detained. Rape is punished by fines set largely by the difference in status between victim and accused. Adultery is treated similarly, with higher fines when it took place in the house of the woman's father, husband or brother; the fine falls on the man, and the code provides no punishment for the woman.</p><p>A divorced woman keeps the property she brought to the marriage and half of the income derived from it. A child conceived before a divorce and born after it belongs to the father and, if he does not accept it, to the mother. A widower holds his wife's property in trust and may not act on it without her children's consent; when a father dies, the land goes to the sons, and a childless widow is compelled to remarry. Adopted children inherit as natural children do, but women may not adopt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The first fragment was discovered in 1857 by Georges Perrot and Louis Thenon. In 1884 the Italian archaeologist Federico Halbherr found four more columns while excavating near a local mill, and with Ernst Fabricius he uncovered eight further columns whose stones had been reused in the foundations of a Roman odeion of the 1st century BCE. The wall has since been partly reconstructed. All the Cretan legal texts are fragmentary and show continuous amendment, and from them scholars have traced the development of the law, notably the diminishing rights of women and the increasing rights of slaves.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gortyn code” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Marc Van De Mieroop suggests that it may have influenced the Greek <b>Gortyn Code</b> and the Roman Twelve Tables, while acknowledging that most Roman law is not similar to it.</blockquote><a href="/work/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Gortyn Code</b>, inscribed c. 450 BCE on a wall later built into the Roman odeion, sets out laws on marriage, divorce, inheritance, adoption and the status of slaves and serfs.</blockquote><a href="/state/gortyn">Gortyn</a></li></ul></section>
